{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-e1-reviewer-fable-24","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:47b6fb6bf1d4fbcefd7c","verdict":"demonstrated_issue","bounded_statement":"Within the supplied files for case:47b6fb6bf1d4fbcefd7c, the selected result is a Welch two-sample t-test computed on all 48 scan-level rows (24 per ink formulation), even though the data description designates electrode_id as the independent unit and states that each of the 12 strips contributes four repeat scans. Each independent unit (strip) therefore enters the row-independent t-test procedure four times, inflating the effective sample size from 6 strips per group to 24 scans per group; the reported df = 43.0 and p < 1e-06 reflect this scan-level entry. The stated conclusion's headline statistics (t = 9.23, df = 43.0, p < 1e-06) are not supported at the declared independent-unit level, although a strip-level reanalysis of the same data (collapsing to per-strip means) still yields a significant GX9 > GX7 difference of 6.15 uA (Welch t ≈ 4.3, df ≈ 9.3, p ≈ 0.002).","root_cause":"The workflow's currents_by_ink function pools every scan row into a flat per-formulation list keyed only by ink_formulation, discarding the electrode_id grouping, and then passes those pooled scan-level lists directly to scipy.stats.ttest_ind. No aggregation to one value per electrode_id (or any mixed/clustered model accounting for within-strip correlation) is performed before the row-independent t-test, so the four repeat scans of each strip are treated as four independent observations.","issue_class":"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure","evidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description declares electrode_id as the independent-unit column and states that rows sharing an electrode_id are repeat measurements of one strip, not separate strips.","observed_value":"Independent unit = electrode_id; 12 strips x 4 scans each = 48 rows","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":10,"end_line":11,"quoted_text":"One row is: a single amperometric scan of one sensor strip, giving the oxidation peak current recorded on that scan\nIndependent unit column: electrode_id"},{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":28,"end_line":30,"quoted_text":"The file is plain ASCII CSV with a header line and 48 data rows. Readings\nfrom the same electrode_id are repeat measurements of one strip rather than\nmeasurements of separate strips."}]},{"description":"The workflow pools all scan rows per ink formulation, discarding electrode_id, and runs the Welch t-test on those pooled scan-level lists, so each strip's four scans all enter the test as separate observations.","observed_value":"ttest_ind applied to lists of 24 scan values per ink (4 per strip)","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":30,"end_line":34,"quoted_text":"def currents_by_ink(scans):\n    grouped = {}\n    for ink, _strip, current in scans:\n        grouped.setdefault(ink, []).append(current)\n    return grouped"},{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":61,"end_line":63,"quoted_text":"outcome = stats.ttest_ind(\n        currents[candidate], currents[reference], equal_var=False\n    )"}]},{"description":"The report explicitly states that each recorded scan was entered as one observation in the t-test, and its table shows 24 scans versus 6 strips per formulation.","observed_value":"24 scans per group entered; only 6 independent strips per group","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":18,"end_line":20,"quoted_text":"Each recorded scan was entered as one observation and the two formulations\nwere compared on peak current with a two-sided Welch two-sample t-test\n(unequal variances)."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":13,"end_line":14,"quoted_text":"| GX7 | 24 | 6 | 43.17 | 2.59 |\n| GX9 | 24 | 6 | 49.32 | 1.98 |"}]},{"description":"The selected result reports df = 43.0, which is only attainable with ~24 observations per group; a strip-level analysis (6 per group) could yield at most df = 10, confirming the test was run at scan level with each strip entering four times.","observed_value":"t = 9.23, df = 43.0, p < 1e-06 at scan level; strip-level reanalysis gives Welch t ≈ 4.33, df ≈ 9.3, p ≈ 0.002","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":27,"end_line":27,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Welch two-sample t-test on scan-level peak current: GX9 (49.32 uA) exceeds GX7 (43.17 uA) by 6.15 uA, t = 9.23, df = 43.0, p < 1e-06."}]},{"description":"The input data confirm four consecutive rows per electrode_id with nearly identical peak currents (e.g., GS-01: 41.6, 42.3, 41.9, 42.2), showing strong within-strip correlation among the repeated entries.","observed_value":"Within-strip scan range ~0.7 uA versus between-strip spread of several uA","source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":2,"end_line":5,"quoted_text":"GS-01,GX7,1,41.6,24.9\nGS-01,GX7,2,42.3,24.9\nGS-01,GX7,3,41.9,25.0\nGS-01,GX7,4,42.2,25.0"}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"The report is transparent about the clustered design: it discloses that each strip was scanned four times, reports the strip counts (6 per formulation) alongside the scan counts, and labels the test as scan-level. This shows the repeated entry was disclosed rather than hidden, and the workflow does track electrode identity (strips_by_ink) for the summary table — but neither fact changes that the inferential procedure itself treated the 4 scans per strip as independent observations.","observed_value":"Design disclosed; strip counts reported; but no strip-level inference performed","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":6,"end_line":9,"quoted_text":"with one of two carbon ink formulations, GX7 and GX9. Each strip was mounted\nin the flow cell once and scanned four times in immediate succession against a\nfixed ferrocyanide standard; the oxidation peak current of each scan was\nrecorded in microamperes (uA)."},{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":37,"end_line":41,"quoted_text":"def strips_by_ink(scans):\n    grouped = {}\n    for ink, strip, _current in scans:\n        grouped.setdefault(ink, set()).add(strip)\n    return grouped"}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I attempted to falsify the finding in three ways. (1) I checked whether the workflow aggregates to one value per electrode_id before testing: currents_by_ink demonstrably discards the strip identifier and appends every scan value to a flat per-ink list, and strips_by_ink is used only for the descriptive table, so no aggregation occurs. (2) I checked whether the reported df could be consistent with a strip-level test: with 6 strips per group the maximum possible Welch df is 10, but the report states df = 43.0, which is only consistent with ~24 observations per group, confirming scan-level entry. (3) I checked whether the conclusion would be unchanged under a correct unit-level analysis: computing per-strip means from data/input.csv and running a Welch t-test on 6 vs 6 strip means gives t ≈ 4.33, df ≈ 9.3, p ≈ 0.002 — still significant in the same direction, but materially different from the reported t = 9.23, df = 43.0, p < 1e-06, so the specific selected result as stated is not supported at the independent-unit level. None of these attempts overturned the finding.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]}